 Setting up a Linux VM from MyCloud
 ----------------------------------
 First Steps after provisioning
 Add User
 
 RedHat:
 ------
 useradd -G wheel kweinertrun passwd after creating the user.passwd kweinert
 
 Set NOPASSWD to run sudo without a password.
 sudo visudo%wheel  ALL=(ALL)   	NOPASSWD: ALLAdd existing user to wheel group >usermod -G wheel
 Using sed:
 sudo sed -i -e 's/^%wheel/# %wheel/' -e '/NOPASSWD/s/^#%wheel/%wheel/' /etc/sudoers
 
 Note there are a couple new Red Hat clones available now that Centos is no longer in the game.Rocky Linux  Designed to be 100% bug-for-bug compatible with Red Hat Enterprise Linux  AlmaLinux binary compatible with RHELOracle Linux  Another  RedHat compatible OS (this one has wsl images available as well)
 
 Ubuntu: 
 -------
 adduser kweinertusermod -aG sudo kweinertvisudo:%sudo   ALL=(ALL) NOPASSWD:ALL
 Another Ubuntu option is  Xubuntu I've been using this version for local VMS. It uses the Xfce desktop by default instead of Gnome.This makes it a little more light weight but still has a desktop  when needed.Using an SSH Keyssh key for automatic login various terminals.
 Create the key
 From Windows Terminal with OpenSSH
 >ssh-keygenCreates id_rsa & id_rsa.pub in the userhome/.ssh folderid_rsa is the private key and goes on the from host. On Windows it gets added to a storage area with ssh-add command.id_rsa.pub goes into authorized_keys file on each host you want to log into.On Linux the id_rsa and authorized_keys need to have -rw------- permissions (chmod 600)And the .ssh folder needs  drwx------. (chmod 700)If it is set incorrectly the login will silently switch to a password login.
 Troubleshooting
 Red Hat 
 tail -f -n 50 /var/log/secure | grep sshd
 Ubuntu
 tail -f -n 50 /var/log/auth.log | grep 'sshd'
 Error suggesting ssh file permissions: Authentication refused: bad ownership or modes for file /home/kweinert/.ssh/authorized_keys
 Troubleshoot ssh from the client:
 ssh -v  host (more Vs = more verbose output)
 
 
 Registering a RedHat VM
 -----------------------
 Obtain the required account on RedHat (I forget how I think it is through an incident in SNOW)
 Registration process:
  Log into the system.[username@hostname~]$ sudo subscription-manager register[sudo] password for username:Registering to: subscription.rhsm.redhat.com:443/subscriptionUsername: subscription_name (Subscription credentials provided by the Support Lab team on the incident)Password:The system has been registered with ID: 59c3f606-e9a7-495c-afab-b82a5eaac887The registered system name is: COMP-4843-1[username@COMP-4843-1 ~]$sudo sudosubscription-manager attach --auto[sudo] password for username:Successfully attached a subscription for: Red Hat Middleware, Self-Support (NFR, Partner Only)
 Deregistration process:
 [username@COMP-4843-1 ~]$ sudo subscription-manager unregister[sudo] password for username:Unregistering from: subscription.rhsm.redhat.com:443/subscriptionSystem has been unregistered.[username@COMP-4843-1 ~]$ sudo subscription-manager cleanAll local data removedP-4843-1 ~]$ sudo subscription-manager register
 removed
 
 
 Adding the extra disk to Red Hat
 --------------------------------
 Using LVM (Add the new disk to the existing root partition)
 lsblk
   (sdb is the new disk)
 	NAME          MAJ:MIN RM  SIZE RO TYPE MOUNTPOINT
 	sda             8:0    0   60G  0 disk
 	├─sda1          8:1    0    1G  0 part /boot
 	└─sda2          8:2    0   59G  0 part
 	├─rhel-root 253:0    0   37G  0 lvm  /
 	├─rhel-swap 253:1    0  3.9G  0 lvm  [SWAP]
 	└─rhel-home 253:2    0 18.1G  0 lvm  /home
 	sdb             8:16   0   50G  0 ????? <I forget what it showed here.
 	sr0            11:0    1 1024M  0 rom
 	G
 sudo pvcreate /dev/sdb
 
 sudo vgs
   (get name of volume group (rhel on this system)
 	VG   #PV #LV #SN Attr   VSize   VFree
 	rhel   1   3   0 wz--n- <59.00g 4.00m
 
 sudo vgextend rhel /dev/xvdc
 
 sudo vgs 
  (note the new size)
 
 sudo vgdisplay
  (note the Free size on /)
 
 sudo lvs
   (choose the correct logical volume (root))  
 	LV   VG   Attr       LSize
 	home rhel -wi-ao----  18.08g
 	root rhel -wi-ao---- <37.04g
 	swap rhel -wi-ao----  <3.88g
 	
 sudo lvextend -l +100%FREE /dev/rhel/root
   (extend the LV)
 
 sudo xfs_growfs /dev/rhel/root
 	(use the extra space)
 
 df -h
   (verify increase)
   Before:
     /dev/mapper/rhel-root   38G   18G   20G  50% /
   After:
 	/dev/mapper/rhel-root   87G   18G   70G  21% /
 
 
 Using fdisk (Create a new partition and mount it)
 ------------
 https://linuxconfig.org/how-fstab-works-introduction-to-the-etc-fstab-file-on-linux
 sudo fdisk -l
 locate the new disk in the list. (mine was Disk /dev/sdb: 50 GiB)
 sudo fdisk /dev/sdb
 Opens the disk with fdisk
 Hit n for new partition
 Hit p for primary
 keep defaults on the next few questions
 partition number 1
 first sector 2084
 last sector x bytes based on available gigs of space.
 Type w to write the changes to disk
 exit fdisk at this point.
 
 fdisk -l to get the new disk info.
 run
 sudo mkfs -t ext4 /dev/
 sudo mkdir -p /local/mount/point
 sudo mount /dev/sdb1 /local/mount/point
 
 add to fstab
  lsblk -d -fs /dev/sdb1
 NAME FSTYPE LABEL UUID                                 MOUNTPOINT
 sdb1 ext4         c79caeea-2a1d-4f45-bd79-38d05cf8b551 /data
 
 UUID=c79caeea-2a1d-4f45-bd79-38d05cf8b551 /data ext4    defaults   0      2
